    "content": "## Inside the Forest Citadels of the Kenyan Coast\n\nAlong the wooded ridge behind Mombasa, Kilifi, and Kwale stand the **Sacred Kaya Forests** of the **Mijikenda** (*Midzi Chenda* — literally *\"The Nine Homesteads\"*). In his *Introductory Note* to *Giryama Vocabulary and Collections* (1891, pp. v–vii), Rev. William E. Taylor mapped the Giryama homeland around the central fortress of **Kaya Fungo (*Bomani*)** into **three territorial divisions and 70 named districts (*malalo*, sg. *lalo*)**: **Weruni** (South-West, 15 districts including *Chijembeni, Miyuni, Mwidzimwiru, Mbuyuni, Birini, Ts'aka Reri, Gulicha*), **Central Giryama** (31 districts including *Kaya Fungo, Mwandoni, Ndatani, Murimani, Kidzini, Mwaeba, Gotani, Kinagoni, Vigongoni*), and **Northern Giryama** (24 districts stretching to **Gohu / Kilima cha K'ilifi**, *Dindiri, Sinikumbe, Rare, K'onjora, Mutangani, Mangea*, and *Garashi*).\n\nBetween 1844 and 1891, three landmark volumes recorded the language, governance, botany, and oral literature of the Mijikenda from within the Rabai and Giriama Kayas:\n- **Rev. Dr. J. L. Krapf's *Vocabulary of Six East-African Languages*** (1850);\n- **Krapf & Rev. Johannes Rebmann's 391-page *A Nika-English Dictionary*** (1887); and\n- **Rev. William E. Taylor's *Giryama Vocabulary and Collections*** (1891, pp. i–xxviii, 1–140) alongside his 64 Giryama and Nyika proverbs in ***African Aphorisms*** (1891).\n\n---\n\n## 1. The 13 *Marika* Circumcision Cycles and the Degrees of *Habasi* & *K'ambi*\n\nIn *Giryama Vocabulary and Collections* (1891, pp. 19, 34–35), Taylor documented the constitutional structure of Giriama society:\n\n1. **The 13 Named Circumcision Cycles (*Marika*, sg. *Rika*, p. 19)**:\n   - Historical chronology was preserved through thirteen grand ruling cycles (*marika*) in exact succession: **1. Wula-mbere** (*\"First-rains\"*), **2. Wula-kahi** (*\"Middle-rains\"*), **3. Wula-nyuma** (*\"Latter-rains\"*), **4. T'ungudza** (*\"Tomatoes\"*), **5. P'uku** (*\"Large wild rats\"*), **6. Nguluwe** (*\"Pigs\"*), **7. Vitambi** (*\"Coloured cloths\"*), **8. Vitsoka** (*\"Hatchets\"*), **9. Abi-Kizi** (*\"Kizi's-father\"*), **10. Atsai** (*\"Wizards\"*), **11. Mafira** (*\"Puff-adders\"*), **12. Mitseka** (*\"Mats\"*), and **13. Nyoga** (*\"Feathers\"* — the cycle current in 1891).\n2. **The Initiatory Degrees of *Habasi* & *K'ambi* (under *Freemasonry*, pp. 34–35)**:\n   - Citizens advanced through graded initiatory lodges (**Habasi**): (1) **Uhoho**, (2) **Umondo**, (3) **Ubora** (comprising *Nyere*, *Muhagizi*, and *Ahoho a Mihasoni*), (4) **Gawe** (comprising *M'kuzi* and *Mwandza M'kulu*, centered on the sacred friction-drum *mwandza*), (5) **Sayo ra K'oma**, (6) **M'ng'aro**, and (7) **Kirao**—before entering the ruling council of the **K'ambi**, where elders received the ivory or horn armlet (**luwoo**) at **Nyambura** and deliberated in the **M'oro** parliament across three supreme orders:\n     - **Waya** (grade **Mwa-M'kulu**, meeting in the **bandari** lodge);\n     - **Fisi** (grade **Mwa-Rima** — *\"The Hyenas\"* — enforcers of solemn oaths and supreme sanctions); and\n     - **Enye-ts'i** (*\"Owners of the Land\"* — the supreme constitutional elders of the Kaya).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Traditional 4-Day Mijikenda Week, 3 Lunar Decades & Agricultural Seasons\n\nTaylor (1891, p. 55 under *Moon* and p. 111 under *Year*) and Krapf (1882, p. 214; 1887) recorded the indigenous time-reckoning of the Mijikenda prior to the coastal 7-day week:\n- **The Three 10-Day Lunar Decades (*Mwezi*, p. 55)**: **Muandamo** (first 10 days of the waxing moon), **Mulungulungu** (middle 10 days of the full moon), and **Kidziza** (last 10 dark days of the waning moon).\n- **The 4-Day Agricultural Week (p. 55)**:\n  1. **Kuramuka**: The first day of going out to cultivate the fields.\n  2. **Kurima-hiri**: The second consecutive cultivating day.\n  3. **Kuisa**: The third cultivating day (*\"finishing\"*).\n  4. **Jumwa**: The fourth day of rest, palm-wine fellowship, market exchange, and council assemblies.\n- **Seasons & Ecological Proverbs (p. 111)**: The solar year (*mwaka*, calculated by the Pleiades **kirimira**, p. 66) opens with **Mwakani** (the great rains, proverb: *Mwaka ni mosi*), followed by **Mutsano** (windy cold season), **Kipahu** (dry season), **Vuri** (lesser rains, proverb: *Vuri k'arina m'singa*), and **Kaskazi** (hot northerly wind, proverb: *Kaskazi m'p'unga-ngulu*).\n\n---\n\n## 3. *Ngano za Kigiryama* (5 Folktales) & *Ndumo za Kigiryama* (22 Riddles)\n\nIn the Collections of *Giryama Vocabulary and Collections* (1891, pp. 114–140), Taylor published **five bilingual Giriama prose texts (*Ngano za Kigiryama*)**—(1) *Katsungula na Simba* (pp. 114–129), (2) *Fisi na Simba na Katsungula* (pp. 130–131, ending with the traditional closing formula *\"Ngano yangu igomera haha: Mwehu nyumba-ni muna ts'azi ya nyaa\"*), (3) *Mwanache na Nzimu* (pp. 131–133), (4) *K'uku na Katsungula* (pp. 133–134), and (5) *Kuandika kwa Kufwa, na Kuononga kwa Muche* (pp. 134–137)—followed by **22 traditional riddles (*Ndumo za Kigiryama*, pp. 138–140, opened with *\"Chondoni!\" — \"Dekeha!\"* p. 76)**:\n\n| Source Ref. | Kigiryama Riddle / Proverb | Literal Translation | Meaning & Cultural Insight |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Riddle #1** | *Baba wákot'a kizimba dzulu ya m'uyu. — Ni p'ula.* | Father nailed a beehive (*kizimba*) upon the baobab tree (*m'uyu*). — It is the nose (*p'ula*). | Compares the human nose on the face to a cylindrical honey-barrel hung high on a baobab. |\n| **Riddle #6** | *Fw-alume ahahu, akiuka mumwenga, k'afurya wari. — Ni mafigo.* | We are three men; if one goes away, we eat no cornmeal (*wari*). — The three hearthstones (*mafigo*). | Every member of a household or council is essential to balance the whole. |\n| **Riddle #8** | *K'akilumika. — Ni kikokora.* | It cannot be bitten. — It is the elbow (*kikokora*). | Even the strongest person has limits and needs others. |\n| **Riddle #18** | *Magoma mairi lwanda-ni. — Ni mahombo.* | Two cows, in the open field (*lwanda-ni*). — It is the breasts (*mahombo*). | Celebrates maternal nourishment using pastoral imagery. |\n| **Riddle #22** | *Nyumba yangu k'aina muryango. — Ni iji.* | My house has no door. — It is an egg (*iji*). | Shared Bantu riddle of unbroken life. |\n| **Proverb #603** | *Chala chimwenga k'achibanda ts'aha.* | One finger cannot crush a louse. | Unity and mutual aid (*kikowa*) are indispensable. |\n| **Proverb #640** | *Mulungu k'afwa, ela at'u ndio afwao.* | God (*Mulungu*) never dies; it is mortal people who pass away. | Divine justice outlives human generations. |\n\n---\n\n### Explore Mijikenda Heritage on KenyaHub:\n- [Learn Mijikenda (Kigiryama / Chidigo Course & Grammar Guide)](/tools/learn/mijikenda/)\n- [Browse the Mijikenda Dictionary](/tools/kenyan-translator/)"
