    "slug": "nandi-seventeen-clans-totems-folktales-enigmas-hollis-1909",
    "title": "The 17 Nandi Clans (Oret), Sacred Animal Totems (Tiondo), 18 Folktales, and 76 Tongōchik Enigmas in A. C. Hollis (1909)",
    "excerpt": "Inside A. C. Hollis & Sir Charles Eliot's 'The Nandi: Their Language and Folk-Lore' (1909): the 17 exogamous clans and their sacred totems, 18 interlinear Nandi folktales, 57 proverbs, and 76 riddles.",
    "author": "KenyaHub Cultural Heritage",
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-26T15:00:00.000Z",
    "updatedAt": "2026-10-30T08:00:00.000Z",
    "tags": [
      "Culture & History",
      "Languages",
      "Kalenjin",
      "Literature"
    ],
    "readTime": 4,
    "sources": [
      {
        "sourceId": "hollis-nandi-1909",
        "title": "The Nandi: Their Language and Folk-Lore (A. C. Hollis & Sir Charles Eliot, 1909)",
        "note": "Genealogical Divisions & Sacred Animals (pp. 4–11), 18 Folk-Tales (pp. 98–123), 57 Proverbs (pp. 124–132), and 76 Enigmas (pp. 133–151)."
      },
      {
        "sourceId": "hobley-akamba-ethnology-1910",
        "title": "Ethnology of A-Kamba and Other East African Tribes (C. W. Hobley, 1910)",
        "note": "Comparative Oret/Korgat clan and totem tables for Elgeyo, Kamasia (Tugen), Sotik, and Lumbwa (Kipsigis) (pp. 157–160)."
      }
    ],
    "content": "## Clan Totemism and Oral Literature in *The Nandi* (1909)\n\nIn *The Nandi: Their Language and Folk-Lore* (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909, pp. 4–11, 312), A. C. Hollis documented the **seventeen genealogical clans (*Oret*, pl. *Ortinwek*)** of the Nandi, recording for each clan its principal name, its public name (used by women and children), its sacred totem animal (**tiondo**), and its specific ecological and dietary taboos.\n\n## 1. Selected Nandi Clans (*Oret*) and Their Sacred Totems (Hollis 1909, pp. 5–11)\n\n| Clan Name (*Oret*) | Public / Women's Name | Sacred Totem (*Tiondo*) | Ecological & Customary Rule (Hollis 1909) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Kipoiis** | *Kaputiei / Kipkenda* | Jackal (*lelwot*) & Cockroach (*solopchot*) | Senior clan; members never harm jackals |\n| **Kipkōkōs** | *Kapsegoi* | Buzzard (*chepkōkōsit*) | Forbidden to eat zebra or rhinoceros meat |\n| **Kipiegen** | *Kapchepkendi* | Baboon (*moset*) & House-rat (*muriat*) | Forbidden to hunt in forests alone |\n| **Tungo** | *Korapor* | Hyena (*kimaketiet*) | Respected as intermediaries between the living and ancestral spirits (*oiik*); hold the rear guard in battle |\n| **Kipaa** | *Kapcherom* | Colobus Monkey (*koroiit*) & Snake (*erenet*) | Women wear colobus-fur ornaments |\n| **Talai** | *Kapsiondoi* | Lion (*ngetundo*) | Clan of the ritual prophets (*Orkoiyot*); hold the right flank in war and wear lion-skin headpieces |\n| **Toiyoi** | *Kapakori* | Rain (*robta*) & Soldier Ant (*pirechet*) | During a thunderstorm, a Toiyoi member thrusts an axe into the hearth ashes and steps outside to calm the storm |\n| **Kipsirgoi** | *Kapchepkoiya* | Bush-pig (*toret*) | Forbidden to overcome a bush-pig unless it raids their eleusine millet field (*imbaret*) |\n\n## 2. Highlights from the 76 Nandi Enigmas (*Tongōchik-ap-Nandi*, pp. 133–151)\n\nHollis (1909) recorded 76 interlinear Nandi riddles (*tongōchik*), asked only after dark:\n- **Enigma #1**: *A-koi ak a po-minan. — Ngotiot-ap-chii ne-oo.* (\"I am tall and yet I have no strength. — The tall elder's walking-spear.\")\n- **Enigma #11**: *A-tinye lakōk pokol ang tukul ko-laotik. — Segemik.* (\"I have a hundred children and all of them wear warrior belts. — Bees in the hive.\")\n- **Enigma #29**: *Ki-a-eny giplelyo ak kipsitye che-ker-key. — Kōret ak barak.* (\"I offered a white ox and a red ox of equal size. — The Earth and the Sky.\")\n- **Enigma #53**: *Kot-ap-koko ikongen tukul. — Konda.* (\"Grandmother's house goes round and round everywhere. — The human eye.\")\n- **Enigma #61**: *Ngiri, ngiri! — Tamirmiriet.* (\"Step for step! — A person's shadow.\")\n\n\n## 3. The Nandi Age-Set Cycle (*Ipinda*) and the *Saket-ap-Eito* Ceremony\n\nAlongside the seventeen exogamous clans (*Ortinwek*), Hollis (1909, pp. 11–13, 52–58) documented the cyclical **seven-age-set system (*Ipinda*, pl. *Ipinwek*)** that governed Nandi and broader Kalenjin civic, military, and generational life across roughly 105-year cycles:\n1. **The Seven Recurring *Ipinwek* Names**: **Maina**, **Chumo**, **Sawe**, **Kipkoimet** (formerly *Korongoro*), **Kaplelach**, **Kimnyige**, and **Nyongi**. Because a son typically entered the age-set two steps after his father, generational memory and historical chronology across the Rift Valley highlands were dated by which *ipinda* held warrior responsibility.\n2. **The Handing-Over Festival (*Saket-ap-Eito*, \"Slaughter of the Bullock\")**: Held approximately every fifteen years, the retiring warrior age-set formally transferred the defense of the country to the newly initiated warrior grade before a white bullock, stepping up into the status of senior statesmen and judicial elders (**Boisiek**) who presided over the parish councils (**Kokwet**).\n\n## 4. The 12-Month Nandi Ecological Calendar (*Arawet*, Hollis 1909, pp. 94–95)\n\nHollis (1909, pp. 94–95) also recorded the indigenous twelve-month lunar and agricultural calendar (**Arawet**, pl. **Arawōk**), divided into the wet cultivating season (*Kipsuunde*) and the dry pastoral season (*Kiptamo*):\n- **Kiptamo (February)**: Hot dry month when land is cleared and prepared for sowing.\n- **Iwat-kut (March)**: Month of the first breaking rains (*\"rain in gusts\"*), when women sow eleusine finger millet (**paiyat / wimbi**).\n- **Mamalik & Maki-irir (April–May)**: Months of heavy long rains and weeding.\n- **Kipsuunde & Kipsuunde-ne-bo-eng' (September–October)**: Harvest months when ripe millet is reaped with small iron knives and stored in raised granaries (**choket**).\n### Explore Kalenjin Heritage on KenyaHub:\n- [Learn Kalenjin (10-Unit / 19-Module Interactive Course)](/tools/learn/kalenjin/)\n- [Explore 72 Verified Kalenjin Proverbs & Riddles](/tools/learn/)\n- [Search 2,806 Verified Kalenjin Dictionary Entries](/tools/kenyan-translator/)"
