    "content": "## The Ateker (Eastern Nilotic) Language of Northwestern Kenya\n\nPublished in the *Journal of the Royal Anthropological Institute* (Vol. 51, 1921, pp. 43–73), **Juxon Barton's *Turkana Grammatical Notes and Vocabulary***—together with **Sir Harry Johnston's *The Uganda Protectorate, Vol. II*** (1902)—provides the earliest comprehensive grammatical and lexical record of **Ng'aturkana** and its close kinship with **Ateso (*Elgumi*)** and **Karamojong**.\n\n## 1. Gender-Number Noun Prefixes in Ng'aturkana & Ateso (Barton 1921, pp. 45–47)\n\nEvery noun in Ng'aturkana carries an obligatory prefix marking both **Gender** (Masculine, Feminine, or Neuter/Diminutive) and **Number** (Singular vs. Plural):\n\n| Gender | Singular Prefix | Plural Prefix | Ng'aturkana Example (Barton 1921) | English Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Masculine** | **E-** (or *Ek-*) | **Ng'i-** (or *Ng'ik-*) | **ekile** → **ng'ikiliok** | man → men |\n| **Masculine** | **E-** | **Ng'i-** | **emong** → **ng'imongin** | ox → oxen |\n| **Feminine** | **A-** (or *Ak-*) | **Ng'a-** (or *Ng'ak-*) | **aberu** → **ng'aberu** | woman/wife → women/wives |\n| **Feminine** | **A-** | **Ng'a-** | **aite** → **ng'atuk** | cow → cattle |\n| **Feminine** | **A-** | **Ng'a-** | **akipi** (pl. tantum) | water |\n| **Neuter / Diminutive** | **I-** (or *Ik-*) | **Ng'i-** | **ikoku** → **ng'ide** | child → children |\n\nnotice that the language name **Ng'aturkana** itself uses the feminine plural prefix **ng'a-** (agreeing with *ng'akiro*, \"words / speech\"), while the people are **Ng'iturkana** (masculine/animate plural prefix **ng'i-**).\n\n## 2. Pastoral Salutations, Dance Oxen (*Emong*) & Ideophones (Barton 1921, pp. 44, 69)\n\nBarton (1921) highlights two vivid features of Turkana speech and pastoral culture:\n- **Pastoral Greetings & Farewells (p. 69)**:\n  - Greeting: **\"Mata! Mata ng'atuk!\"** (\"Greetings! Health to your cattle!\") — Reply: **\"Mata!\"**\n  - Farewell: **\"Tolot-ion!\"** (\"Go thou in peace!\") — Reply: **\"Ejok, alothi!\"** (\"It is good, I go!\")\n- **Rich Cattle & Horn-Shape Lexicon**: Every warrior chants the praises of his favorite **dance ox (*emong lo edonga*)**, whose horns are trained to curve forward, upward, or downward, and whose hide pattern gives the warrior his praise-name (*ekorio* = giraffe-spotted ox, *chapo* = white ox, *emerii* = spotted ox).\n\n\n## 3. Verb Classes (*To-* vs. *Ki-*) and VSO Syntax in Barton (1921)\n\nIn pages 48–54 of *Turkana Grammatical Notes and Vocabulary* (1921), Juxon Barton outlines the morphological engine of **Ng'aturkana** verbs, which mirrors sister Ateker languages (**Ateso**, **Karamojong**, **Toposa**, and **Nyangatom**):\n1. **The Two Morphological Verb Classes (*Ko-/To-* vs. *Ki-*)**:\n   - **Class 1 (*To-/Ko-* Verbs)**: Form their singular imperative with the prefix **to-** (or **ko-** by vowel harmony) and 1st person singular indicative with **a-**: e.g., **to-lot!** (\"go!\"), **a-lothi ayong** (\"I go\"); **to-nyam!** (\"eat!\"), **a-nyami ayong** (\"I eat\").\n   - **Class 2 (*Ki-* Verbs)**: Retain an underlying high-vowel prefix **i-** (imperative **ki-**): e.g., **ki-gyen!** (\"depend/trust!\"), **ki-muj!** (\"eat food!\").\n2. **Verb-Initial (VSO) Word Order & Nominative Tone Marking**: In neutral declarative sentences, the inflected verb stands first, followed by the Subject (marked by Nominative tone reduction) and the Object (marked by Absolutive tone).\n3. **Expressive Ideophones & Ecological Precision**: Barton notes that Turkana narratives employ vivid phonemic ideophones to depict light, motion, and sound across the semi-arid plains of the Suguta, Kerio, and Turkwel (*Tirkwel*) river valleys.\n\n## 4. The Lake Turkana (*Anam Ka'alakol*) & Riverine Ecological Lexicon\n\nBarton's 1921 vocabulary (pp. 55–73) and Johnston's 1902 comparative tables preserve core terminology for life around **Lake Turkana (*Anam*)** and the seasonal gallery forests along the **Turkwel** and **Kerio** rivers:\n\n| Ng'aturkana Term (Barton 1921) | Plural Form | Category | English Meaning & Ecological Context |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Anam** | *Ng'anamin* | Hydrography | Lake Turkana (\"the great lake / sea\") |\n| **Ang'olol** | *Ng'ang'ololin* | Hydrography | Seasonal sandy river (such as the Turkwel or Kerio) |\n| **Akipi** | *(pl. tantum)* | Sustenance | Water (dug from dry riverbed wells, *ng'akare*) |\n| **Edome** | *Ng'idomei* | Flora | Doum palm (*Hyphaene compressa*), whose nuts (*eng'ol*) provide dry-season food and basket fiber |\n| **Ekorio** | *Ng'ikorioi* | Fauna / Heraldry | Reticulated giraffe; also the praise-term for a giraffe-patterned dance ox |\n| **Akiru** | *Ng'akir* | Climate | Rain (associated with the supreme sky deity **Akuj**) |\n### Explore Ng'aturkana & Ateso on KenyaHub:\n- [Learn Turkana (12-Unit / 34-Module Interactive Course)](/tools/learn/turkana/)\n- [Learn Ateso (8-Unit / 15-Module Interactive Course)](/tools/learn/teso/)\n- [Search 1,914 Turkana & 505 Ateso Dictionary Entries](/tools/kenyan-translator/)"
